== Chapter XII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The conception of sin through its deceit. What it consists in. The consent of the will to sin. Its nature. The ways and means by which it is obtained. Other advantages used by the deceit of sin. Ignorance, errors.]
]

#smallcaps[The] third success of the deceit of sin in its advancing work is the #emph[conception of actual sin.] When it has drawn the mind away from its duty and entangled the affections, it goes on to #emph[conceive sin,] with a view to bringing it forth. “Then, when lust has conceived, it gives birth to sin.” Now the #emph[conception of sin,] with a view to committing it, can be nothing but the #emph[consent of the will.] For as sin cannot be committed without the #emph[consent of the will,] so where the will has consented to it, there is nothing in the soul to hinder its actual accomplishment. God does indeed by various ways and means frustrate the bringing forth of these adulterous conceptions. He makes them melt away in the womb, or in one way or another miscarry, so that not the least part of the sin that is #emph[willed] or #emph[conceived] is committed. Yet once the #emph[will] has given its consent, there is nothing left in the soul itself to check it. Often, when a cloud is full of rain and ready to fall, a wind comes and drives it away. And when the #emph[will] is ready to bring forth its sin, God turns it aside by one wind or another. But the cloud was as full of rain as if it had fallen, and the soul was as full of sin as if the sin had been committed.

This #emph[conceiving of lust,] or of sin, then, is its prevailing in obtaining the #emph[consent of the will] to its solicitations. By this the soul is robbed of its chastity toward God in Christ, as the apostle suggests in 2 Cor 11:2, 3. To clear up this matter we must observe:

First, that the #emph[will] is the principle, the nearest seat and cause, of #emph[obedience] and #emph[disobedience.] Moral actions are good or evil to us, or in us, so far as they share in the #emph[consent of the will.] He spoke the truth long ago who said, “Omne peccatum est adeoò voluntarium, ut non sit peccatum nisi sit voluntarium.” “Every sin is so voluntary that if it is not voluntary, it is not sin.” This is most true of #emph[actual sins.] The formal nature of their iniquity arises from the acts of the will in them and concerning them; I mean with regard to the #emph[persons] who commit them. Otherwise, in itself, the formal reason of sin is its #emph[departure from the law of God.]

Secondly, there is a twofold #emph[consent of the will] to sin.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, one that is #emph[full, absolute,] complete, and given on deliberation. It is a prevailing consent: the convictions of the mind have been conquered, and there is no principle of grace in the will to weaken it. With this #emph[consent] the soul goes into sin like a ship before the wind with all its sails spread, without any check or stop. It rushes into sin like the horse into battle. By it men, as the apostle says, “have given themselves over” to sin “with a craving for more” (Eph 4:19). This is how #emph[Ahab’s will] was in the murder of #emph[Naboth.] He did it on deliberation, by plotting, with full consent. Doing it gave him such satisfaction that it cured his #emph[sickness,] the disorder of his mind. This is the #emph[consent of the will] that is acted in the finishing and completing of sin in unregenerate persons. It is not required for the simple bringing forth of sin, which is what we are speaking of.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, there is a #emph[consent of the will] that is accompanied by a secret #emph[resistance] and a #emph[willing] of the contrary. This is how #emph[Peter’s] will was in denying his Master. His #emph[will] was in it, or he would not have done it. It was a #emph[voluntary action,] something he chose to do at that time. The sin would not have been #emph[brought forth] if it had not been #emph[conceived] in this way. Yet at that very time there was present in his will a contrary principle of love to Christ, indeed of faith in him, which did not fail utterly. Its efficacy was intercepted, and its workings actually suspended, through the violent pressure of the temptation he was under. But it was still in his #emph[will,] and it weakened his consent to sin even though he consented. It was not done with the #emph[self-pleasing] that such full acts of the will produce.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, though there may be a #emph[predominant consent in the will,] which may be enough for the conception of particular sins, there cannot be an #emph[absolute, total, full] consent of the will of a believer to any sin. For:]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, there is in his #emph[will] a principle fixed on good, on all good (Rom 7:21): “he wants to do good.” The principle of grace in the #emph[will] inclines him to all good. And in general this prevails against the principle of sin, so that the #emph[will] takes its name from it. In the #emph[will] of every believer grace has the rule and dominion, not sin. Now a consent to sin in the #emph[will] that is contrary to the inclination and general prevailing principle in that same will is not, and cannot be, total, absolute, and complete.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, there is not only a general, ruling, prevailing principle in the will against sin, but there is also a secret #emph[reluctance] in the will against its own act in consenting to sin. It is true that at times the soul does not feel this #emph[reluctance,] because the present consent carries away the prevailing act of the #emph[will] and takes away the sense of the #emph[lusting of the spirit,] the reluctance of the principle of grace in the will. But the general rule holds in all things at all times (Gal 5:17): “the spirit lusts against the flesh.” It does so #emph[actually,] though not always to the same degree or with the same success. And the prevailing of the contrary principle in this or that particular act does not disprove it. It is the same on the other side. There is no acting of grace in the #emph[will] that sin does not lust against. Though that lusting is not felt in the soul, because the contrary acting of grace prevails, it is still enough to keep those actings from being perfect in their kind. So it is with this resistance of grace against the acting of sin in the soul. Though it is not felt in its workings, it is enough to keep that act from being full and complete. And much spiritual wisdom lies in discerning rightly between the #emph[spiritual resistance] of the principle of grace in the will against sin, and the rebukes that conscience gives the soul for sin upon conviction.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, observe that repeated acts of the #emph[consent of the will] to sin may produce a disposition and inclination in it to similar acts, which may bring the will to a #emph[proneness and readiness] to consent to sin at easy solicitations. This is a dangerous condition of soul, and one to be greatly watched against.]

Fifthly, this #emph[consent of the will,] which we have now described, may be considered in two ways.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, as it is exercised about the circumstances, causes, means, and inducements to sin. Secondly, as it concerns this or that #emph[actual sin.] In the first sense there is a consent of the #emph[will] to sin, in virtue and power, in every failure to watch against sin, in every neglect of duty that makes way for it, in every listening to any temptation that leads toward it. In a word, it is in all the diversions of the mind from its duty and all the entanglements of the affections by sin mentioned above. For where there is no act of the #emph[will,] either #emph[formally] or #emph[virtually,] there is no sin. But this is not what we are now speaking of. We are speaking in particular of the #emph[consent of the will] to this or that actual sin, so far that either the sin is committed, or it is prevented by other ways and means that are not now under consideration. And in this consists the #emph[conceiving of sin.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[With these things supposed, what we are to consider next is the #emph[way] the deceit of sin goes about obtaining the #emph[consent of the will,] and so conceiving actual sin in the soul. To this purpose, observe:]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, that the #emph[will is a rational appetite]: rational as it is guided by the mind, and an appetite as it is stirred by the affections. So in its working or actings it has regard to both and is influenced by both.]

Secondly, it chooses nothing and consents to nothing except #emph[sub ration boni,] as it has an #emph[appearance of good,] some present good. It cannot consent to anything under the notion or apprehension of its being #emph[evil] in any way. #emph[Good] is its natural and necessary object. So whatever is put before it for its consent must be put before it with an appearance of being good, either #emph[in itself,] or good to the soul at #emph[present,] or good in the circumstances it is in. So:

Thirdly, we may see from this the reason why the #emph[conception of sin] is placed here as a consequence of the #emph[mind’s being drawn away] and the #emph[affections’ being entangled.] Both of these have an influence on the #emph[consent of the will,] and so on the conception of this or that actual sin. So our way here is made somewhat plain. We have seen at length how the mind is drawn away by the deceit of sin and how the affections are entangled; what remains is only the proper effect of these things. To uncover it, we must give some instances of the special #emph[deceits,] the corrupt and false reasonings mentioned earlier, and then show how they prevail on the #emph[will] to consent to sin.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, the #emph[will] is imposed on by the corrupt reasoning that #emph[grace is exalted in pardon,] and that mercy is provided for sinners. As has been shown, this first deceives the mind, and that opens the way to the will’s consent by removing the sight of the #emph[evil] the will has an aversion to. In carnal hearts this prevails so far as to make them think that their #emph[freedom consists in being slaves of corruption] (2 Pet 2:19). And its poison often taints and corrupts the minds of believers themselves; that is why we are so warned against it in the Scripture. So to what has been said before about the use and abuse of the doctrine of the grace of the gospel, we will add a few more considerations, and fix on one place of Scripture that will give light on it. There is a twofold #emph[mystery] of grace: of walking with God, and of coming to God. The great design of sin is to alter the doctrine and #emph[mystery] of grace with regard to these things by applying to the one the considerations proper to the other. By this each part is hindered, and the influence of the doctrine of grace on them, which would further them, is defeated. See 1 John 2:1, 2: “I am writing these things to you#super[+] so that you#super[+] will not sin. But if anyone does sin, we have an advocate before the Father, Jesus Christ, the Righteous One. He Himself is the propitiation for our sins.” Here the whole design and use of the gospel is briefly expressed. “These things,” he says, “I am writing to you#super[+].” What were these things? Those mentioned in 1:2: “this is the life that was revealed; we have seen it and testified to it, and we proclaim to you#super[+] the eternal life that was with the Father and was revealed to us,” that is, the things concerning the person and mediation of Christ; and in 1:7, that pardon, forgiveness, and atonement for sin are to be obtained by the blood of Christ. But to what end and purpose does he write these things to them? What do they teach? What do they tend to? A universal abstaining from sin. “I am writing to you#super[+],” he says, “so that you#super[+] will not sin.” This is the proper, only, genuine end of the doctrine of the gospel. But to abstain from all sin is not our condition in this world (1 John 1:8): “If we say we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us.” What, then, is to be done in this case? Supposing sin, supposing that we have sinned, is no relief provided for our souls and consciences in the gospel? Yes, he says: “if anyone does sin, we have an advocate before the Father, Jesus Christ, the Righteous One. He Himself is the propitiation for our sins.” There is full relief in the propitiation and intercession of Christ for us. This is the order and #emph[method] of the doctrine of the gospel and of applying it to our own souls: first, to keep us from sin; and then, to relieve us against sin. But here the deceit of sin comes in and puts this “new wine into old wineskins,” so that the wineskins burst and the wine is lost as far as our benefit from it goes. It changes this #emph[method] and order of applying gospel truths. It takes up the last first, and that shuts out the use of the first entirely. “If anyone sins, pardon is provided” is all the #emph[gospel] sin would willingly let stay on the minds of men. When we would come to God by believing, sin would be pressing the first part, being #emph[free from sin,] when the gospel chiefly sets forth the second, the pardon of sin, for our encouragement. When we have come to God and should walk with him, sin will have only the second part set forth, that there is pardon of sin, when the gospel chiefly sets forth the first, keeping ourselves from sin, since the grace of God has appeared to us, bringing salvation, to that end and purpose. Now the mind, entangled with this deceit, drawn away from its watch by it, and diverted from the true ends of the gospel, imposes on the #emph[will] in several ways to obtain its consent.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, by a sudden #emph[surprise] in a case of temptation. Temptation is the representation of something as a #emph[present good,] a particular good, when it is a real evil, a general evil. Now when a temptation armed with opportunity and provocation befalls the soul, the principle of grace in the #emph[will] rises up to reject and detest it. But suddenly the #emph[mind,] deceived by sin, breaks in on the #emph[will] with a corrupt and false reasoning from gospel grace and mercy. This first staggers the will’s opposition, then weakens it, and then makes the will tip the scale by its consent on the side of the temptation, which presents evil as a present good. And so sin is conceived in the sight of God, though it is never committed. In this way the seed of God is sacrificed to #emph[Molech,] and the weapons of Christ are abused in the service of the devil.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it does it imperceptibly. It slips in the poison of this corrupt reasoning little by little, until it has greatly prevailed. The whole effect of the doctrine of the gospel in holiness and obedience consists in the soul’s being cast into its frame and mold (Rom 6:17). In the same way the whole of #emph[apostasy] from the gospel is chiefly the casting of the soul into the mold of this false reasoning: that sin may be indulged on account of grace and pardon. By this the soul is gratified in sloth and negligence, and drawn away from its care about particular duties and avoiding particular sins. The mystery of the law of grace is to look for salvation as if we had never performed any duty, since after we have done everything we are unprofitable servants, resting on sovereign mercy through the blood of Christ; and to attend to duties with all diligence as if we looked for no mercy, that is, with no less care, though with more liberty and freedom. This reasoning imperceptibly works the soul away from that mystery. The deceitfulness of sin tries by every means to work the soul away from it, and by this it corrupts the #emph[will] when its consent is required to particular sins.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, the deceived #emph[mind] imposes on the #emph[will] to obtain its consent to sin by setting before it the advantages that may come and arise from it; this is one #emph[means] by which the mind itself is also drawn away. It makes what is #emph[absolutely evil] a present #emph[apparent good.] So it was with #emph[Eve] (Gen 3). Laying aside all consideration of the law, covenant, and threatenings of God, she at once reflects on the advantages, pleasures, and benefits she would obtain by her sin, and counts them up to solicit the consent of her will. The tree “is,” she says to herself, “good for food and pleasing to the eyes, and … desirable for obtaining wisdom.” What should she do, then, but eat it? Her will consented, and she did so. The pleas for obedience are laid out of the way, and only the pleasures of sin are taken into consideration. So #emph[Ahab] says (1 Kgs 21): “Naboth’s vineyard is next to my palace, and I may use it as a vegetable garden”\; therefore I must have it. A deceived mind imposed these considerations on his #emph[will] until it made him obstinate in pursuing his covetousness through perjury and murder, to the utter ruin of himself and his family. In this way the guilt and tendency of sin are hidden under the cover of advantages and pleasures, and so sin is conceived, or resolved on, in the soul.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, as the #emph[mind,] when it is #emph[drawn away,] so also the #emph[affections,] when they are #emph[enticed] and entangled, greatly further the conception of sin in the soul by the #emph[consent of the will.] They do it in two ways.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, by some hasty #emph[impulse and surprise.] When the affections themselves are stirred up, provoked, and drawn out by some violent provocation or suitable temptation, they throw the whole soul, as it were, into an uproar, and draw the will into consenting to what they are provoked to and entangled with. Such was the case of #emph[David] in the matter of #emph[Nabal.] A violent provocation, from the extremely unworthy behavior of that foolish churl, stirs him up to wrath and revenge (1 Sam 25:13). He resolves on it, to destroy a whole family, the innocent with the guilty (vv. 33, 34). Self-revenge and murder were for that time conceived, resolved on, and consented to, until God graciously drew him back. His entangled, provoked affections surprised his #emph[will] into consenting to the conception of many bloody sins. The case was the same with #emph[Asa] in his #emph[anger,] when he struck the prophet, and with #emph[Peter] in his #emph[fear,] when he denied his Master. Let the soul that would take heed of conceiving sin take heed of entangled affections. For sin may be #emph[suddenly] conceived; the prevailing consent of the will may be suddenly obtained, and this gives the soul a fixed guilt, though the sin itself is never actually brought forth.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, enticed affections obtain the #emph[consent of the will by frequent solicitations,] by which they gain ground on it imperceptibly and enthrone themselves. Take as an instance the sons of #emph[Jacob] (Gen 37:4). They #emph[hate] their brother because their father loved him. Their affections being enticed, many new occasions arise to #emph[entangle] them further, such as his #emph[dreams] and the like. This lay rankling in their hearts and never stopped soliciting their #emph[wills] until they resolved on his death. The unlawfulness and unnaturalness of the act, the grief of their aged father, the guilt of their own souls, are all laid aside. The hatred and envy they had conceived against him did not stop until they had got the consent of their wills to his ruin. The wise man describes excellently this gradual progress of the prevailing of corrupt affections in soliciting the soul to sin (Prov 23:31, 32, 33, 34, 35). And this is the common way sin proceeds in the destruction of souls who seem to have made some good commitments in the ways of God. When it has entangled them with one temptation and brought the will to some liking of it, that at once becomes another temptation, either to neglect some duty or to refuse more light. And commonly the thing by which men fall away from God entirely is not the thing they were first entangled with. This may be enough, briefly, for the third #emph[advancing act] of the deceit of sin. It obtains the #emph[will’s consent] to its conception, and by this means multitudes of sins are #emph[conceived in the heart] that defile the soul very little less, and make it contract very little less guilt, than if they were actually committed.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[To what has been said about the #emph[deceitfulness] of #emph[indwelling sin] in general, which greatly shows its #emph[power] and efficacy, I will add, to close this discourse, one or two particular ways in which it acts deceitfully. These consist in advantages it makes use of, and in means of relieving itself against the search made after it by the word and Spirit for its ruin. We will name here only one head of each sort.]

First, it takes great advantage of the #emph[darkness of the mind] to work out its designs and intentions. The #emph[shadows of a mind] that is totally dark, that is, utterly without saving grace, are the proper workshop of sin. That is why its effects are called the “deeds of darkness” (Eph 5:11; Rom 13:12), as springing from there. Sin works and brings forth with the help of darkness. The working of lust under the cover of a #emph[dark mind] is, as it were, the upper region of hell, for it lies next door to hell in filth, horror, and confusion. Now there is a #emph[partial darkness] still remaining in believers; they “know in part” (1 Cor 13:12). Though there is a principle of saving light in all of them, and the morning star has risen in their hearts, yet not all the #emph[shadows of darkness] are utterly driven out of them in this life. And there are two parts, as it were, or two chief effects, of the #emph[remaining darkness] in believers. First, #emph[ignorance,] or not knowing the will of God, either #emph[juris] or #emph[facti]: of the rule and law in general, or of how the particular act before the mind relates to the law. Secondly, #emph[error] and mistakes, positively taking for truth what is falsehood, and for #emph[light] what is #emph[darkness.] Now the #emph[law of sin] takes great advantage of both of these to exert its power in the soul.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, if there is any remaining ignorance of anything of the will of God, sin will be sure to make use of it and improve it to the uttermost. Though #emph[Abimelech] was not a #emph[believer,] he was a person who had a #emph[moral integrity] in his ways and actions. He declares that he had it, in a solemn appeal to God, the searcher of all hearts, even in the matter in which he went wrong (Gen 20:5). But he did not know that #emph[fornication] was a sin, or so great a sin that it was unfitting for a morally honest man to defile himself with it. So lust hurries him into that intention of evil with regard to #emph[Sarah,] as we have it related there. God complains that his people “are destroyed for lack of knowledge” (Hos 4:6). Being ignorant of the mind and will of God, they rushed into evil at every command of the law of sin. Whether it is a duty to be performed or a sin to be committed, if there is darkness or ignorance of mind about it, sin will not lose its advantage. Many a man who does not know the duty that lies on him for the #emph[instruction of his family,] and throws the whole weight of it on the public teaching, is brought by the #emph[deceitfulness of sin] into a habitual sloth and negligence of duty. As much #emph[ignorance] of the will of God and of duty as there is, so much advantage is given to the law of sin. And from this we may see what the true knowledge is that is acceptable to God. How exactly many a poor soul walks with God, though he is low in notional knowledge! It seems such souls know so much that sin has little advantage against them on that account. Others, meanwhile, high in their notions, give advantage to their lusts even by their #emph[ignorance,] though they do not know it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, #emph[error] is a worse part or effect of the mind’s darkness, and it gives great advantage to the law of sin. There is indeed #emph[ignorance] in every #emph[error,] but there is not #emph[error] in all #emph[ignorance,] and so they may be distinguished. I need to illustrate this with only one consideration: that of men who, being zealous for some #emph[error,] seek to suppress and persecute the truth. Indwelling sin desires no greater advantage. How it will pour out wrath, revilings, and harsh speeches every day, every hour, and breathe revenge, murder, and desolation, perhaps under the name of zeal! On this account we may see poor creatures pleasing themselves every day, as if they were boasting of their excellence, when they are foaming out their own shame. Under their real darkness and pretended zeal, sin sits securely, and fills pulpits, houses, prayers, and streets as full as they can hold with the bitter fruits of envy, malice, wrath, hatred, evil suspicions, and false speaking. The common outcome with such poor creatures is that the holy, blessed, meek Spirit of God withdraws from them, and leaves them visibly and openly to the evil, perverse, wrathful, worldly spirit that the law of sin has cherished and heightened in them. Sin dwells nowhere more securely than in such a frame. So, I say, it lays hold of particular advantages to practice on with its deceitfulness, and in them also to exert its power in the soul. This single instance, its improving the darkness of the mind to its own ends, is sufficient evidence of it.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it uses means of relieving itself against the pursuit made after it in the heart by the word and Spirit of grace. I will name one of its wiles of this kind also, by way of instance, and that is the #emph[lightening of its own guilt.] It pleads for itself that it is not so bad, so filthy, so fatal as is claimed. It goes on in this course of extenuation in two ways. First, #emph[absolutely.] It will have many secret pleas that the evil it tends toward is not so destructive as conscience is persuaded it is; it may be ventured on without ruin. It will urge these considerations strongly when it is at work by way of surprise, when the soul has no leisure or freedom to weigh its suggestions in the balance of the sanctuary. And not seldom the #emph[will] is imposed on by this, and sin gains the advantage of slipping out from under the sword of the Spirit. “It is not such a sin that it cannot be let alone, or allowed to die of itself, which it will probably do before long. There is no need for the violence that is to be used in mortification. It is time enough to deal later with a matter of no greater importance.” And there are other pleas like those mentioned before. Secondly, #emph[comparatively,] and this is a large field for its deceit and subtlety to lurk in. “Though it is indeed an evil to be given up, and the soul is to be made watchful against it, yet it is not of that size and degree we may see in the lives of others, even saints of God, much less of the kind some saints of old have fallen into.” By these and similar pretenses, I say, it seeks to escape and keep its home in the soul when it is pursued to destruction. And how small a portion of its deceitfulness is it that we have declared!]
